+---
+type: spec
+task_slug: ratelimit
+status: approved
+created: 2026-10-15
+tags: [timeparse, spec, python, rate-limiting, token-bucket]
+test_framework: pytest
+interview_rounds: 0
+lane: light
+summary: "Token-bucket RateLimiter in timeparse/ratelimit.py with exact-when-due semantics"
+---
+
+# SPEC: ratelimit
+
+## 🎯 Intent
+Callers of `timeparse` need a way to throttle work to "N per duration" using the same duration grammar the package already parses. A token bucket gives bursts plus a sustained rate, and the ask pins down edge semantics (a token is usable exactly when due) that naive float arithmetic gets wrong.
+
+## 🌅 Outcomes
+A caller can build `RateLimiter("10/1s", burst=5)`, ask `allow()` whether to proceed, and ask `retry_after()` how long to wait, with deterministic behavior under an injected clock.
+
+## 📋 In-Scope Scenarios
+
+### AC-001: Construction validates its arguments
+**Given** any `rate`, `burst`, `clock` arguments
+**When** `RateLimiter(rate, burst, clock)` is constructed
+**Then** `"10/1s"`, `"5/1m"`, `"3/1h30m"` with `burst=1` succeed, and every invalid input raises `ValueError`: a non-string rate, no `/`, more than one `/`, `N` not an ASCII-digit positive integer (`"0/1s"`, `"-1/1s"`, `"1.5/1s"`, `"/1s"`), a duration `parse_duration` rejects (`"10/"`, `"10/xyz"`), a zero duration (`"10/0s"`), and a `burst` that is not an `int` or is `<= 0` (`0`, `-1`, `1.5`, `True`), and a non-callable `clock`. `burst` is required; `clock` defaults to `time.monotonic` and is a zero-argument callable returning float seconds, first read at construction.
+
+### AC-002: Bucket starts full and drains by burst
+**Given** `RateLimiter("1/1h", burst=3)` and a fixed clock at 0.0
+**When** `allow()` is called four times
+**Then** the first three return `True` and the fourth returns `False`; `allow(3)` on a fresh limiter returns `True` and an immediately following `allow()` returns `False`.
+
+### AC-003: A token is available exactly when due
+**Given** `RateLimiter("10/1s", burst=1)` with a controllable clock
+**When** `allow()` is called at clock readings 0.0, 0.1, 0.2, … 1.0 (each computed as the user wrote them: 0.1 steps, including `0.3` and values whose float difference is below 0.1)
+**Then** every call returns `True`; and a call at 0.05 after the call at 0.0 returns `False`.
+**And** availability is judged with a tolerance of 1e-9 tokens, and a successful take clamps the level at `>= 0`, so rounding error never accumulates.
+
+### AC-004: Refill is continuous and capped at burst
+**Given** `RateLimiter("3/1h30m", burst=3)` drained to 0 at clock 0.0 (rate = 3 tokens / 5400 s)
+**When** the clock reads 1799.0, 1800.0, and then 10**9
+**Then** `allow()` at 1799.0 is `False`; at 1800.0 is `True`; after 10**9 seconds idle exactly `burst` (3) consecutive `allow()` calls succeed and the 4th fails.
+**And** a clock reading lower than the previous one is treated as zero elapsed time (no tokens added or removed) and the stored last-seen time is not lowered, so readings 10, 5, 10 credit 0 s in total, not 5 s.
+
+### AC-005: retry_after reports seconds until n tokens are due
+**Given** `RateLimiter("10/1s", burst=1)` after `allow()` at 0.0
+**When** `retry_after()` is called at 0.0, 0.05, and 0.1
+**Then** it returns `0.1`, `0.05` (each within 1e-9) and `0.0`; on a fresh limiter it returns `0.0`; for `RateLimiter("3/1h30m", burst=3)` drained at 0.0, `retry_after(1)` is `1800.0` and `retry_after(3)` is `5400.0`; the return type is `float`.
+
+### AC-006: Failed allow and retry_after never take tokens
+**Given** two identical limiters A and B sharing one clock, any nondecreasing clock sequence, and any sequence of calls
+**When** A additionally receives denied `allow(n)` calls and any `retry_after(n)` calls that B never receives
+**Then** every later `allow` / `retry_after` result on A equals B's, and at any instant `retry_after(n) == 0.0` exactly when `allow(n)` would return `True`.
+
+### AC-007: n is validated by both methods
+**Given** `RateLimiter("10/1s", burst=3)`
+**When** `allow(n)` or `retry_after(n)` is called with `n` of `0`, `-1`, `4`, `1.5`, `"1"`, or `True`
+**Then** each raises `ValueError`, and `n` of `1`, `2`, `3` is accepted; a rejected call takes no tokens.
+
+## 🚫 Non-Goals
+- Thread safety / locking, async support, blocking `wait()` / `acquire()`.
+- Per-key limiters, persistence, or sharing state across processes.
+- Fractional or non-integer token counts for `n`, `burst`, or `N`.
+- CLI exposure or changes to `parse_duration`.
+- Sub-1e-9-token precision guarantees.
+
+## ⚠️ Constraints
+| Constraint | Value | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|
+| Test framework | `pytest` | Already used by `tests/` and `pyproject.toml` |
+| Performance | O(1) per call | Token bucket needs no history |
+| Security | none | No I/O, no untrusted deserialization |
+| Compatibility | Python version of `pyproject.toml`; stdlib only | No new dependency |
+| Module | `timeparse/ratelimit.py`, class `RateLimiter` | Given in the ask |
+
+## 🔒 Irreversible Decisions
+none
+
+## ✅ Verification Criteria
+| Scenario | Verification mode | Test name / manual step |
+|---|---|---|
+| AC-001 | unit | `tests/test_ratelimit.py::test_ac001_construction_validation` |
+| AC-002 | unit | `tests/test_ratelimit.py::test_ac002_starts_full_and_drains` |
+| AC-003 | unit | `tests/test_ratelimit.py::test_ac003_exactly_when_due` |
+| AC-004 | unit | `tests/test_ratelimit.py::test_ac004_refill_and_cap` |
+| AC-005 | unit | `tests/test_ratelimit.py::test_ac005_retry_after_values` |
+| AC-006 | unit (property, twin-limiter) | `tests/test_ratelimit.py::test_ac006_no_side_effects` |
+| AC-007 | unit | `tests/test_ratelimit.py::test_ac007_n_validation` |
+
+Oracles: AC-001/002/004/005/007 are `golden` (values come from the user's ask and hand arithmetic: 3/5400 s = 1 per 1800 s). AC-003 is `golden` (the user's stated schedule). AC-006 is `property` (twin-limiter equivalence holds for any implementation).
+
